  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 214 PM CST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nUpper ridge dominates the Central CONUS into the Great Lakes region \nas a upstream Pacific trough shifts east into the Inter-Mountain \nWest. Gusty winds observed up to 45 mph persist into the afternoon \nas RH values drop down to around 30 percent for 1-2 hours in the \nFlint Hills. While fuels are beginning to cure, humidity values are \nnot expected to fall below 30 percent for either today or Wednesday \nafternoon.  With the persistent warming trend and similar setup on \nWednesday, would not be surprised to see a few hours of elevated \nFire Danger in the afternoon for portions of east central Kansas. \n\nA northern stream upper trough is progged to lift over the northern \nplains Wednesday night. Deep moisture from the Gulf remains shunted \nsouth and east, keeping any measurable precipitation negligible \n(<10%) through Friday afternoon.  Persistent southerly WAA and \nboundary layer mixing will push afternoon highs from the middle 60s \nWednesday to near 70 degrees Thursday and Friday. \n\nThe large-scale upper trough is anticipated to deepen across the \nRockies into the Central Plains on Saturday, driving a strong cold \nfront through northeast Kansas in the afternoon and early evening \nperiods. Ensembles are beginning to converge on more widespread \nprecip probabilities along the front, peaking between 35 to 45% \nSaturday afternoon. Cluster analysis reveals a near 50/50 split on \ntiming and QPF amounts with this system, ranging from at least 0.5 \ninch up to over an inch of rain by late Saturday. \n\nBehind the front, a much colder airmass returns highs back into the \n40s and low 50s Sunday into early next week.  Given the mostly clear \nskies and good radiational cooling, overnight forecast lows are down \nto the low 30s Tuesday morning. A secondary southern stream \ndisturbance may bring light showers, perhaps mixed with some snow \n(?) on Tuesday. Ensemble means however are very limited on moisture \navailability so overall confidence is low.",
